{
  "diagnosis": "The fused softmax kernel was both functionally correct and significantly faster than the incumbent two-pass decomposition. The measured median runtime nearly halved, with logical bandwidth rising from ~89 GB/s to ~172 GB/s, validating the hypothesis that intermediate S buffer traffic was the primary bottleneck. Masking and reduction invariants were correctly handled for the irregular N=781, as confirmed by the output validation. No resource or numerical issues were observed—register and shared memory usage increased only modestly (registers/thread rose from 17–21 to 31, shared bytes constant at 16). All outputs were covered and correct.",
  "outcome": "The fused row-parallel softmax kernel eliminated redundant memory traffic and improved cache locality, nearly doubling throughput. The transformation succeeded and is confirmed as both correct and substantially faster than the incumbent structure.",
  "next_step": "With memory bandwidth now much less of a bottleneck, further improvements could explore increasing kernel compute intensity or better vectorization. This could involve splitting very wide rows into multiple program instances (if N were much larger), or introducing blockwise reductions for even larger shapes. For this case, however, the current configuration is close to optimal. Alternative directions could include experimenting with num_warps/num_stages or using fp16 accumulation for even greater bandwidth efficiency if tolerances permit."
}

