{
  "diagnosis": "The row-chunked decomposition kernel compiled and executed, but achieved no measurable speedup over the incumbent fused kernel. The measured runtime (median_ms ~0.0184) matches the single-row-per-program baseline, and logical bandwidth is unchanged. This is expected for N=781: each chunked program instance must redundantly load both chunks to compute the correct row-wide normalization (max/sum), so no memory traffic is saved and no additional parallelism is gained for this moderate width. Output coverage, reduction, and masking were correct, and no numerical or resource errors were observed.",
  "outcome": "The chunked decomposition is correct and contract-compliant but brings no throughput improvement for N=781. Both the incumbent and this variant saturate available parallelism and memory bandwidth. The redundant loading and reduction inherent to chunked normalization becomes beneficial only for much larger N, where single-program-row kernels run out of registers or do not saturate GPU occupancy.",
  "next_step": "No further chunking or parallel decomposition is likely to yield additional speedup for this shape. For N ~ 781, the fused row-parallel softmax is optimal. If larger N shapes are of interest, test chunked/blockwise variants there. Otherwise, further attempts should explore different numerical strategies (e.g., fp16 accumulation, aggressive in-register fusion) only if tolerances allow, or experiment with kernel launch parameters (num_warps/stages), though major gains are unlikely."
}

