{
  "diagnosis": "The blockwise two-chunk-per-row decomposition kernel was contract-compliant and functionally correct, but it was slower than the incumbent fused row-parallel kernel. The measured median runtime increased to ~0.0194 ms (vs. incumbent ~0.0175 ms), and logical bandwidth dropped to ~165 GB/s. This is expected: for N=781, each program instance redundantly loads both 512-wide blocks to correctly compute the row-wise normalization, so memory traffic is increased and no additional useful parallelism is exposed. Output coverage and masking were correct, and no resource or numerical errors were observed. The only effect of this decomposition for this shape is increased redundant memory traffic and kernel launch overhead.",
  "outcome": "The attempted blockwise decomposition is correct but counterproductive for this problem size. Redundant loading for global normalization adds memory traffic and slight kernel overhead, resulting in a measurable regression compared to the incumbent fused kernel. No throughput or efficiency gain is realized, and the best structure for N=781 remains the single-row-per-program fused softmax kernel.",
  "next_step": "Do not pursue further chunked/blockwise variants for this shape, as the incumbent fused row kernel is optimal for N~781. If targeting much larger N, blockwise reductions may become beneficial. For this case, alternate directions could include exploring fp16 accumulation (if tolerances allow), experimenting with num_warps or num_stages, or attempting in-register fusions. Otherwise, further chunked decompositions are not justified for the current problem size."
}
